1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,320
Hi, I'm George Georgopoulos.

2
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:06,480
My MSc thesis at the
University of Piraeus asks:

3
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:12,440
does a language model write more specific,
better-supported security requirements, if

4
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:15,600
it also reads the product's
own documentation?

5
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:19,240
To answer it, I built SecReqGen.

6
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:22,940
You give it a user story,
for example from Mattermost:

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,840
"As an admin, I want to invite guest
users so external collaborators can access

8
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:34,040
specific channels." You also give it
a short description of the system.

9
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:39,020
It searches the product's documentation
for the words of the story, and gives the

10
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:40,740
best pages to the model.

11
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:48,100
The model writes security requirements,
grouped by the chapters of OWASP ASVS, and

12
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:52,260
is asked to cite the passage
behind each product detail.

13
00:00:53,019 --> 00:00:55,420
It can also draft a threat list.

14
00:00:55,460 --> 00:01:01,360
Each threat has a threat actor,
a STRIDE category and a quote from the

15
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:04,640
documentation, and a
second model checks it.

16
00:01:05,099 --> 00:01:10,960
A browser interface shows each step of
a run, and puts each threat beside its

17
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,420
quote, so a person can
keep it or delete it.

18
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:21,120
How did I test it? On open-source products
like Keycloak, Bitwarden and Mastodon,

19
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:25,200
the same stories ran with and
without the documentation.

20
00:01:25,540 --> 00:01:31,320
I counted the requirements that name a
product part from a list made in advance.

21
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:36,240
And AI reviewers checked a sample
of requirements against the sources.

22
00:01:36,839 --> 00:01:43,020
A Bitwarden login story asks the app to
store its tokens safely, but not where.

23
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:49,940
Without the documentation, one answer put
a token in "a designated managed account

24
00:01:49,980 --> 00:01:53,700
data area", which an
engineer still has to find.

25
00:01:53,740 --> 00:01:59,580
With the documentation, an answer named
AccountTokens, where the manual says the

26
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:05,640
tokens are stored. On 15 stories,
the documentation raised the share of

27
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:12,480
requirements with a listed name by
45 points for Qwen and 51 for Gemma,

28
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:14,700
both statistically significant.

29
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:20,160
After several rounds of research and
tests, I improved the search and the

30
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:27,060
instructions. On 14 new stories, specific,
supported requirements rose from 53

31
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:34,160
to 85 of 100. On 50 feature requests
from ten open-source projects,

32
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:40,080
97 of 100 requirements were
usable, against 63 before.

33
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:46,460
And on every project, a smaller share
was wrong, unsupported or unusable.

34
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:52,980
So, with its own documentation, a language
model writes more specific security

35
00:02:53,020 --> 00:02:53,854
requirements.
